          "text": "Warning: truncated output (original token count: 10016)\nTotal output lines: 334\n\nBridge Geometry Manual (https://www.fhwa.dot.gov/bridge/pubs/hif22034.pdf)\nciteturn1view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 236; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn0search12\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 6400\nL0@P0: Bridge I\nL1@P0: CL Horizontal\nL2@P0: Alignment\nL3@P0: PC(l)\nL4@P0: 119+56. 79 ~\nL5@P0: (])\nL6@P0: 8+83.96\nL7@P0: Pl(l)\nL8@P0: 133+83.96\nL9@P0: 0\nL10@P0: 191 .\nL11@P0: CURVE 2 CURVE 3\nL12@P0: PT(]) 202+24.77\nL13@P0: East(X)\nL14@P0: U.S. Department of Transportation\nL15@P0: Federal Highway Administration\nL16@P0: Bridge Geometry Manual\nL17@P0: \nL18@P0: Publication No. FHWA-HIF-22-034\nL19@P0: Infrastructure Office of Bridges and\nL20@P0: Structures\nL21@P0-1: April 2022FOREWORD\nL22@P1: Documenting a bridge’s geometry accurately on bridge layouts and detailed drawings\nL23@P1: during the design process is fundamental to successful bridge construction. Detailed bridge\nL24@P1: geometry provides the information necessary to establish analytical models for bridge\nL25@P1: superstructures and substructures. Geometric constraints often dictate the type of bridge\nL26@P1: to be built at a specific site. As such, determining bridge geometry is central to the work of\nL27@P1: bridge engineers and technicians.\nL28@P1: The purpose of this manual is to provide bridge engineers and technicians with a basic\nL29@P1: framework for evaluating and computing the various components of bridge geometry. The\nL30@P1: manual includes practical examples for implementing the topics discussed. Reference is\nL31@P1: also made to numerous outside resources for further direction and discussion. The manual\nL32@P1: is organized into three Parts including General Topics, Concrete Topics, and Steel Topics.\nL33@P1: While separation among Parts maintains a focused approach, discussion topics inherently\nL34@P1: overlap 